wasix-libc: permission bits from the host that keeps them
(Infrawrench/Infrawrench#959).

Against wasix-org/wasix-libc 67b2eccf (v2026-07-30.1). Ours, and not one for
upstream as it stands: it reads a protocol of the Infrawrench CI runner.

WASI has no file modes. `filestat` has no field for one, `chmod`, `fchmod`
and `fchmodat` return 0 having done nothing, `umask` is a no-op and `stat`
fills in only the file type, so every program built on this library reads
permission bits of 0 and cannot set any. The runner keeps modes beside its
file systems and serves them through control files in /proc/iwci (`mode/`
by path, `ino/` by st_ino). With this patch:

- `stat`, `lstat`, `fstatat` (relative to the working directory or with an
  absolute path) and `fstat` fill in the permission bits of a regular file or
  directory by asking for `ino/<st_ino>` after the real call. The host finds
  a file it has a mode for without walking its path; any other file, most of
  them, costs one failed open and gets the default for its type (0755 for a
  directory or a file directly in /bin, /usr/bin or /usr/local/bin, 0644
  otherwise). A symlink reads 0777 by `lstat`.
- `chmod` and `fchmodat` record a mode by path (and now fail with ENOENT on
  a missing path, as they should). `fchmod` goes by the path this process
  created the file at, if it did, otherwise by st_ino, which only finds a
  file the host already has a mode for.
- `open` with O_CREAT and `mkdir` record their mode, less the umask, when it
  is not the default, so `mkstemp` gives 0600 and `mkdtemp` 0700.
- `umask` keeps a mask (022 to start) for those two.

Without /proc/iwci (checked once per process) all of it behaves as before.
Relative to a directory descriptor other than the working directory there is
no path to give, so `fstatat` and `fchmodat` behave as before there too.

